# Native n8n routing compatibility

Automation implements Switch 3.4 and Merge 3.2 alongside its bounded native Manual Trigger / Set / If / HTTP Request / Wait runtime. The application executes its own source-assisted implementation. Released n8n modules execute only inside the authenticated test harness, and are never the application runtime. Full product parity remains false.

The complete release binding is n8n, n8n-workflow, n8n-core and n8n-nodes-base **2.16.0**, source commit `6eb2d75670b912bdc0274abd73f4f6e8ca8678c6`. [Original manifest](routing-original-manifest.json) identifies 202 exact entries: 201 selected source/package/license files and the nodes-base archive retained locally. The public preservation stores source as non-executable snapshots and excludes that archive. The frozen baseline manifests authenticate all four registry archives, their SHA512 integrities, the dependency lock and the 4,350 retained dependency source/JSON files before and after original executions. [Source option inventory](routing-source-option-inventory.json) records 118 static parameter definitions, defaults, options and display conditions from all retained Switch/Merge released versions; generated filter schemas and dynamic spreads are not a complete generated UI schema.

Official [Switch documentation](https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/builtin/core-nodes/n8n-nodes-base.switch/) and [Merge documentation](https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/builtin/core-nodes/n8n-nodes-base.merge/) were consulted on 2026-10-12. These pages track current documentation rather than an immutable 2.16.0 revision; exact release behavior is bound to the retained package bytes.

| Released version family | Source contract | This stage |
|---|---|---|
| Switch 1 | Four fixed outputs; legacy typed string/number/date rules; numeric fallback | Preserved on import, execution rejected |
| Switch 2 | Dynamic legacy rules; expression output count; labels; scalar/date/regex comparisons | Preserved, rejected |
| Switch 3 / 3.1 / 3.2 / 3.3 | Modern rules and expression modes; historical filter versions/type validation; 3.3 makes output count a literal UI setting | Preserved, rejected |
| Switch 3.4 | Filter version 3, first/all matches, fallback, expression index | Bounded subset below |
| Merge 1 | Legacy append, key match/remove/merge, index, multiplex, pass-through and wait | Preserved, rejected |
| Merge 2 / 2.1 | Two inputs, append, field/position/multiplex combination and wait-for-both branch selection; 2.1 relaxes missing-field behavior | Preserved, rejected |
| Merge 3 / 3.1 | Multiple inputs, append/position/branch selection, field/cartesian joins and SQL; 3.1 SQL item linking | Preserved, rejected |
| Merge 3.2 | Same families with empty SQL-query result behavior | Bounded non-SQL subset below |

Switch supports rules with explicit filter options (`caseSensitive`, `typeValidation`, `version`) and AND/OR conditions from the existing native string, number, boolean, array, object and existence comparisons. Date and regular-expression operators remain unsupported. It supports first match or every matching output, no fallback, an extra fallback, or a numeric existing output. Renamed output labels are preserved in JSON; the connection controls show numeric ports. Expression mode accepts a literal output index or a whole-value `={{ $json.path }}` expression and a literal output count from 1 through 100. The released numeric conversion is retained within the integer port bounds. Empty input returns one empty port. Rules with no configured entries discard items and return one empty port.

Merge supports append and Choose Branch `waitForAll` with 2–10 inputs, selecting an input or producing one empty item. Position combination supports 2–10 inputs and optional unpaired rows. Cartesian combination and field joins support exactly two inputs. Field joins support all five released join modes, outputs from both/input1/input2, simple comma-separated fields or advanced field pairs, literal keys when dot notation is disabled, all/first matches, and the bounded JSON strict/fuzzy comparison behavior. Duplicate advanced destinations use the last lookup value, matching the released helper. Clash behavior supports prefer-last, permitted preferred inputs, suffix keys, deep/shallow combination and override-empty. Field joins accept prefer-input1/prefer-last/suffix; other preferences are rejected. The released position suffix behavior includes the unsuffixed preferred entry, and prefer-input1 Cartesian behavior reverses iteration order. Immediate `pairedItem` input/index links are retained, including the released position helper's duplicate preferred link.

Merge rejects recursive literal `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype` keys. It also rejects results with named properties on arrays because saved JSON cannot retain them. These are intentional deviations, recorded separately from exact matches. Inputs and branches are privately cloned: the original helpers' internal alias effects are reproduced within private data for the tested combinations, but caller-owned inputs are isolated. General JavaScript identity/alias equivalence and non-JSON object graphs are not claimed.

Execution is manual, acyclic, and uses the released `v1` ordering model for the supported graph. When a Merge is reachable, every non-Merge input and every Merge input port allows one incoming edge, and each configured Merge port must connect to a reachable source. Repeated batches, loop generations and ambiguous fan-in are rejected. Inactive branches are not forced. Partial append/combine joins can drain when no executable node remains and no ancestor join is waiting. Choose Branch requires its first two inputs. Port buffers survive Wait checkpoints; Wait output routing is deferred until resume. Public runs/history omit join buffers and the deferred-route marker. Terminal persisted records clear those private buffers. The resume validator ties pending and join states to the same preflighted enabled Manual-trigger graph and rejects unreachable, duplicate, already-executed, satisfied, empty or contradictory routing queues.

Existing bounds remain: 2 MiB workflow, 1 MiB value/item, 8 MiB execution data, 10,000 items, 100,000 structural entries, depth 40, 100 nodes, 300 edges, 500 invocations, 120 seconds active time and 60 seconds Wait. Switch adds a 10,000 aggregate output-item cap, an 8 MiB output cap checked before each branch clone, and 1,000,000 condition evaluations. Merge caps Cartesian output before allocation and field comparisons/combination iterations at 1,000,000 work units; nested value traversal is separately bounded structurally. Both nodes check cancellation and active deadlines during synchronous loops. Data-dependent rejection stops subsequent effects but cannot undo an earlier HTTP request. HTTP's existing request/body/deadline limits remain unchanged.

The native editor can add Switch/Merge, select dynamic source outputs and explicit Merge target inputs, remove the exact selected connection, and load a routing example with Switch and Wait feeding Merge. Unknown JSON fields, node types, options and connections remain preserved on import/export; a reachable unsupported setting prevents execution. The actual isolated browser check exercised those controls, conflicting fan-in validation, save and the six-step run. Its proof is [recorded separately](results/routing-source-study/stage/ui-observations.json). This was the stage test host, not the shared production host or the released n8n editor.

The [production integration receipt](results/routing-validation.json) separates exact output comparisons, error-presence comparisons, dangerous-key rejections, array-representation rejections, helper/block executions and application tests. `node scripts/verify-automation-routing.mjs` from the Studio directory records exact tested sources, raw logs, original observations and pre/post fingerprints in a new results folder. Original differential tests themselves write detailed synthetic observations only under the ignored local oracle folder and emit a small hash/count marker. The focused verifier then preserves those outputs. The historical source validator accepted a disconnected unsupported node in `pending`, and state creation selected a disabled first Manual Trigger; exact failing sources/probes remain preserved. Current creation and continuation validation use the same preflighted enabled Manual graph. The actual shared-host recovery test rejects unreachable imported pending state before HTTP scheduling.

The preserved historical stage browser evidence predates the final enabled-trigger selection correction in the server-side runtime. Its exact UI modules remain current, and the production focused tests cover the corrected runtime, shared HTTP recovery, Wait/restart and the disabled-trigger regression. A fresh production browser validation is separate parent-owned evidence. The receipt binds the historical browser observation to that earlier tested source snapshot rather than implying a fresh browser run against the correction.

Original evidence is deliberately limited. The actual released Switch `execute` runs with a real Workflow expression engine and the exact core cleanup, filter extraction and schema validation helpers. Merge comparisons execute the complete released mode-helper functions with explicit static parameters and released fallback defaults; they do not execute the complete Merge class/router, generated UI/default schema or SQL loader. Core enqueue methods and the exact waiting-input drain block are extracted with authenticated AST ranges and executed with explicit materialized ports/parameters; full WorkflowExecute, server, database, editor, Wait orchestration and deployed n8n processes are not run. Their checks are not counted as node output comparisons. The production suite runs the existing 31 native regression groups unchanged, plus 13 routing groups and 12 released-original groups. The routing persistence tests use the actual shared host and disk store, re-open that host, retain one run ID, execute Merge once, and issue exactly two downstream fixture requests. A separately cancelled Wait remains cancelled after another restart; unreachable imported state rejects with zero downstream requests. Only synthetic owned temporary files and loopback HTTP endpoints are used.

Full product parity remains false: legacy node versions, SQL/AlaSQL, arbitrary expressions/code, providers, credentials/OAuth, binary attachments, webhooks/schedules, published workflows, queues, accounts, collaboration, full error/retry policies, cycles, partial executions, source-overwrite links and complete n8n scheduling/editor/server behavior remain gaps. Original error text/class equality, production security, reliability, performance and superiority are not established. Failed experiments and exact pre-correction sources remain under [the preservation record](results/routing-source-study/preservation-manifest.json); the receipt describes their corrections.
